index dc10423..17ed9c7 100644
--- a/_posts/2021/2021-08-17-speed-up-ggplot.md
+++ b/_posts/2021/2021-08-17-speed-up-ggplot.md
@@ -41,14 +41,14 @@ Today, I finally got tired of it and went down a rabbit hole of DDG searches (ye
 Let's start by generating a dataset of 1 million X and Y coordinates, normally
 distributed:
 
-{% highlight R %}
+```r
 require(data.table)
 pdata = data.table(x=rnorm(1e6), y=rnorm(1e6))
-{% endhighlight %}
+```
 
 How long would the default R `plot()` and `ggplot` methods take to plot this?
 
-{% highlight R %}
+```r
 system.time(with(pdata, plot(x, y)))
 
 user system elapsed
@@ -59,7 +59,7 @@ system.time(print(ggplot(pdata, aes(x=x, y=y)) + geom_point()))
 
 user system elapsed
 13.331 0.220 13.552
-{% endhighlight %}
+```
 
 And here is our starting point: R would take around 11.5 s and `ggplot` even longer,
 with ~13.6 s.
@@ -70,12 +70,12 @@ One of the tips I found on the web comes from [a StackOverflow answer]{: target=
 recommending to use the `pch='.'` option to plot data points as non-aliased single
 pixels.
 
-{% highlight R %}
+```r
 system.time(print(ggplot(pdata, aes(x=x, y=y)) + geom_point(pch='.')))
 
 user system elapsed
 2.688 0.100 2.787
-{% endhighlight %}
+```
 
 This provides a ~5x speed up, from 13.6 s to less than 3 s!
 
@@ -86,14 +86,14 @@ his new R package `scattermore` (last commit to the package was on Jan 31st, 202
 time of writing this post), which uses a C script to rasterize the dots as a bitmap and
 then plot them with R.
 
-{% highlight R %}
+```r
 require(scattermore)
 system.time(print(ggplot(pdata, aes(x=x, y=y)) +
 geom_scattermore()))
 
 user system elapsed
 0.987 0.060 1.047
-{% endhighlight %}
+```
 
 The overall speed up now is of ~13x: from 13.55 s to ~1 s!
 
